WEBVTT

q0001
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.455
When can a larger expression safely collapse to one tiny row-column pair?

q0002
00:00:06.455 --> 00:00:15.811
This episode has one job: Pair-aware collapse is an experimental shortcut for the precise boundary of one live row variable and one live column variable after fixed bindings, with exact fallback otherwise.

q0003
00:00:15.811 --> 00:00:25.167
We build on CM-IR nodes, sharing, and roots, Live support versus ambient variables; their definitions stay fixed while this lesson adds a new layer.

q0004
00:00:25.167 --> 00:00:34.523
The pair-aware shortcut requires exactly one live row variable and one live column variable after fixed inputs are applied.

q0005
00:00:34.523 --> 00:00:42.475
Watch for the label pair-aware path; it stays attached to the artifact this episode means.

q0006
00:00:42.475 --> 00:00:50.926
When we say one live row variable, the highlight identifies its layer before we interpret it.

q0007
00:00:50.926 --> 00:01:00.282
The term one live column variable receives its own visual state so it cannot drift into a neighboring concept.

q0008
00:01:00.282 --> 00:01:06.238
We will keep fallback visible whenever its definition controls the inference.

q0009
00:01:06.238 --> 00:01:15.188
The next sequence uses Four-variable truth layout as a conceptual teaching example, not as measured benchmark evidence.

q0010
00:01:15.188 --> 00:01:19.148
Our stable example is Four-variable truth layout.

q0011
00:01:19.148 --> 00:01:28.504
F(A,B,C,D)=(A AND B) XOR (C OR D), row variables A,B, column variables C,D, MSB-first ordering.

q0012
00:01:28.504 --> 00:01:35.957
Carry assignments, live support, explicit CM, packed truth vectors, and exact output across episodes.

q0013
00:01:35.957 --> 00:01:43.410
We now construct the Four-variable truth layout and name every part before interpreting it.

q0014
00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:52.766
Apply fixed bindings until exactly one live variable remains on each axis and construct the eligible two-by-two tile.

q0015
00:01:52.766 --> 00:02:01.716
Add a third live variable, refuse the shortcut, and forward the unchanged problem to the standard path.

q0016
00:02:01.716 --> 00:02:11.072
Pair-aware collapse applies only after fixed assignments leave one live row variable and one live column variable; otherwise it forwards to the standard path.

q0017
00:02:11.072 --> 00:02:17.028
Read that statement only within this scope: current experimental pair-aware implementation.

q0018
00:02:17.028 --> 00:02:20.988
Its declared measurement boundary is construction eligibility.

q0019
00:02:20.988 --> 00:02:24.948
The uncertainty field says experimental implementation status.

q0020
00:02:24.948 --> 00:02:34.304
The nearest confusing lesson is Eager and lazy CM paths; it owns eager construction timing, while this episode owns pair eligibility.

q0021
00:02:34.304 --> 00:02:41.757
Pair eligibility is experimental and local; ineligible cases must fall back without changing semantics.

q0022
00:02:41.757 --> 00:02:48.711
Decide whether three support/fixed-binding cases are pair-eligible before the fallback arrow appears.

q0023
00:02:48.711 --> 00:02:58.067
One live variable per axis makes the pair path possible; everything else stays on the exact fallback path.

q0024
00:02:58.067 --> 00:03:02.526
Now isolate pair eligibility inside Four-variable truth layout.

q0025
00:03:02.526 --> 00:03:10.977
The example definition and output meaning stay fixed while only the state for pair eligibility changes.

q0026
00:03:10.977 --> 00:03:19.428
That matched before-and-after view assigns the visible consequence to pair eligibility, not to a substituted workload.

q0027
00:03:19.428 --> 00:03:25.384
Row and column support counters shrink independently to one and one.

q0028
00:03:25.384 --> 00:03:30.342
Now isolate 2-by-2 token-pair collapse inside Four-variable truth layout.

q0029
00:03:30.342 --> 00:03:39.292
The example definition and output meaning stay fixed while only the state for 2-by-2 token-pair collapse changes.

q0030
00:03:39.292 --> 00:03:48.242
That matched before-and-after view assigns the visible consequence to 2-by-2 token-pair collapse, not to a substituted workload.

q0031
00:03:48.242 --> 00:03:55.695
The eligible axes snap into a two-by-two token tile and compute all four cases.

q0032
00:03:55.695 --> 00:04:00.154
Now isolate fallback boundary inside Four-variable truth layout.

q0033
00:04:00.154 --> 00:04:08.605
The example definition and output meaning stay fixed while only the state for fallback boundary changes.

q0034
00:04:08.605 --> 00:04:17.056
That matched before-and-after view assigns the visible consequence to fallback boundary, not to a substituted workload.

q0035
00:04:17.056 --> 00:04:23.511
An ineligible third variable diverts through a clearly labeled exact fallback arrow.

q0036
00:04:23.511 --> 00:04:32.867
The example fixes F of A, B, C, and D as A AND B, exclusive-or C OR D.

q0037
00:04:32.867 --> 00:04:38.324
Holding that element fixed lets this episode isolate pair eligibility.

q0038
00:04:38.324 --> 00:04:47.680
A and B select the row, C and D select the column, and MSB-first order fixes both index conventions.

q0039
00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:53.636
Holding that element fixed lets this episode isolate 2-by-2 token-pair collapse.

q0040
00:04:53.636 --> 00:05:02.992
Every four-bit assignment names exactly one of the sixteen cells, and every cell returns one exact output bit.

q0041
00:05:02.992 --> 00:05:08.449
Holding that element fixed lets this episode isolate fallback boundary.

q0042
00:05:08.449 --> 00:05:16.401
Folding the truth-table output column into a four-by-four grid changes the layout, not the function.

q0043
00:05:16.401 --> 00:05:25.757
Reading a cell forward evaluates an assignment; reading it backward recovers the row and column bits that selected it.

q0044
00:05:25.757 --> 00:05:35.113
A common mistake is this: Any subexpression with two total variables is automatically pair-eligible regardless of axis placement.

q0045
00:05:35.113 --> 00:05:40.570
Repair it by returning to the owned distinction: pair eligibility.

q0047
00:05:43.689 --> 00:05:53.045
Use the episode's central distinction to answer: One live variable per axis makes the pair path possible; everything else stays on the exact fallback path.

q0048
00:05:53.045 --> 00:05:59.999
The shortcut is defined by its boundary and fallback, not by its name.
